1. The $K$-Coupons Variant
Condition: Instead of one coupon, the traveler has k discount coupons. Each coupon can be used once on different flights.
•	Code Change: Change the distance array to dist[n+1][k+1].
•	Logic: When traversing an edge, you have two choices if you have coupons left:
1.	Don't use a coupon: dist[v][used] = min(dist[v][used], dist[u][used] + c)
2.	Use a coupon (if used < k): dist[v][used+1] = min(dist[v][used+1], dist[u][used] + c/2)

2. The "Buy One, Get One Free" Variant
Condition: The traveler has a coupon that makes one flight completely free ($cost = 0$), rather than half price.
•	Code Change: In the transition logic where the coupon is used:
o	dist[v][1] = min(dist[v][1], cur_cost + 0);

3. The Round-Trip Variant
Condition: The traveler must go from Dhaka to London and back to Dhaka. The single coupon can be used once at any point in the entire circular journey.
•	Code Change: 1. Run the Dijkstra as you have it to find dist1[i][state] (Dhaka to all). 2. Run another Dijkstra dist2[i][state] starting from London (London to all). 3. The answer is the minimum of dist1[n][0] + dist2[1][1] (coupon used on way back) or dist1[n][1] + dist2[1][0] (coupon used on way there).
4. The "Taxed" Flight Variant
Condition: Every time you land at an airport, you must pay a fixed airport tax $T_i$. The coupon halves the flight price but not the airport tax.
•	Code Change:
o	dist[v][0] = min(..., cur_cost + c + tax[v])
o	dist[v][1] = min(..., cur_cost + (c/2) + tax[v])


