2. Longest Path in a Graph (No Positive Cycles)
Sometimes a problem asks for the "maximum score" or "longest path" instead of the shortest.
•	The Constraint: The graph must not have a positive cycle (a cycle where the sum of weights is $> 0$), otherwise the path could be infinitely long.
•	The Logic:
o	Initialize distances to a very small number (-INF).
o	Flip the comparison: if (d[u] + w > d[v]) { d[v] = d[u] + w; }.
o	This is often used in Project Management (PERT charts) to find the minimum time needed to complete a project.
